"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
1721,"**
Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban al-Misri: narrated to us Yahya ibn Bukayr: narrated to us al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah: narrated to me 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas.

And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Hamzah: narrated to us Yazid ibn Khalid — he is Ibn Mawhab: narrated to us al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah: from 'Ayyash ibn 'Abbas al-Qitbani: from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj: from Nafi': from Ibn 'Umar: from Hafsah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) said,

""Upon every post-pubescent person, attending the Friday prayer is obligatory, and upon whoever attends the Friday prayer, performing the ghusl (ritual bath) is recommended.""

Abu Bakr [ibn al-Arabi] said: This wording — ""Upon every post-pubescent person, attending the Friday prayer is obligatory"" — is an example of the principle we uphold: that when a command is based on an effective cause (*'illah*), it is permissible to draw an analogy (*tamthil*) and liken other cases to it, provided that the effective cause remains. Thus, the command becomes obligatory because the Prophet (ﷺ) stipulated that attending Friday prayer is obligatory upon the post-pubescent person due to puberty being attained. Therefore, whenever puberty is attained — even without seminal emission (*ihtilam*) — and puberty is established through other means, the obligation of attending Friday prayer is binding upon every adult, even if their puberty was not through seminal emission.

Had this not been our principle, and had we followed the principle of those who oppose us in analogy and likeness, and claimed that a command is not based on an effective cause but is purely an act of devotion (*ta'abbud*), then a person who reaches twenty or thirty years of age, is sane, and hears the Friday call to prayer in the city or is at the door of the mosque, would not be obligated to attend Friday prayer unless he had a seminal emission. For the Prophet (ﷺ) only specified that attending Friday prayer is obligatory upon the post-pubescent person, yet many people live for many years without ever experiencing seminal emission.

This is like the saying of Allah, the Exalted:

*""And when the children among you reach puberty, let them ask permission...""* (Quran 24:59)

Allah, the Exalted, only commanded those who have reached puberty to seek permission, for puberty is attainment (*bulugh*). Had it not been permissible to apply analogy and likeness, then a person who reaches thirty years of age without experiencing seminal emission would not be obligated to seek permission.

This is similar to the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ):

*""The Pen has been lifted from three...""*

In the narration, he (ﷺ) said:

*""...and from the child until he reaches puberty.""*

Thus, whoever does not experience seminal emission but reaches an age of discernment without it — the Pen is not lifted from him. For the Prophet (ﷺ) intended by his statement *""until he reaches puberty""* that puberty is attainment, so whenever attainment (*bulugh*) is established — even without seminal emission — the ruling applies to him, and the Pen continues to record his deeds, just as it does after seminal emission.

---"
